Additional observations from the literal results:

- Denial is enforced per-resolved-path before any I/O, with distinct prefixes for the two device classes: `containment: read path …` / `containment: write path …` for `read`/`write`, and a blanket `containment: bash is denied for subagent contained-worker` for `bash`. The `bash` gate is role-based, not path-based — it fired identically for step 4 (an append into the other worktree) and step 8 (a pure read-only `shasum`), so shell access is unavailable to this contained worker regardless of target.
- Steps 2, 3, 6 and 7 were all evaluated as path containment failures, including the symlink case (step 6, resolved to an outside target) and the `..` traversal case (step 7). Neither indirection bypassed the check.
- No step was skipped after an earlier failure; every requested operation was issued and independently rejected.
